  'At 2.4 GHz a quarter wave is about 31 mm, which is why chip and meander antennas are practical there and why an 868 MHz node needs a visibly long whip at about 86 mm. Getting the length wrong by 10% shifts resonance well outside a narrow band and can easily cost 10 dB, which is a factor of three in range.': 'At 2.4 GHz a quarter wave is about 31 mm, which is why chip and meander antennas are practical there and why an 868 MHz node needs a visibly long whip at about 86 mm. Getting the length wrong by 10% shifts resonance well outside a narrow band and can easily cost 10 dB, which is a factor of three in range.',
