  'E series': 'E series',
  'E24 is the IEC 60063 preferred series, 24 values per decade for 5% parts, nominally `10^(n/24)` rounded to two figures. The nearest value is picked on log distance rather than on ohms, because tolerance is a ratio: 62 Ω is as far from 65 Ω as 68 Ω is, in percent.': 'E24 is the IEC 60063 preferred series, 24 values per decade for 5% parts, nominally `10^(n/24)` rounded to two figures. The nearest value is picked on log distance rather than on ohms, because tolerance is a ratio: 62 Ω is as far from 65 Ω as 68 Ω is, in percent.',
  'E48 and E96 are exactly that rounding. E6, E12 and E24 are not: IEC 60063 keeps the historical 27, 33, 39, 47 and 82 where the arithmetic gives 26.1, 31.6, 38.3, 46.4 and 82.5. That is why E24 has a 13 to 15 gap worth 7.1% while its grade is only 5%.': 'E48 and E96 are exactly that rounding. E6, E12 and E24 are not: IEC 60063 keeps the historical 27, 33, 39, 47 and 82 where the arithmetic gives 26.1, 31.6, 38.3, 46.4 and 82.5. That is why E24 has a 13 to 15 gap worth 7.1% while its grade is only 5%.',
  'Each half is a plain voltage divider, so the taps sit at `Vin·R2/(R1+R2)` and `Vin·R4/(R3+R4)`. The bridge output is their difference, `Vout = Vin·(R2/(R1+R2) - R4/(R3+R4))`, which is zero when `R1/R2 = R3/R4`, i.e. `R1·R4 = R2·R3`. Balance depends on ratios only, so it is immune to supply drift.': 'Each half is a plain voltage divider, so the taps sit at `Vin·R2/(R1+R2)` and `Vin·R4/(R3+R4)`. The bridge output is their difference, `Vout = Vin·(R2/(R1+R2) - R4/(R3+R4))`, which is zero when `R1/R2 = R3/R4`, i.e. `R1·R4 = R2·R3`. Balance depends on ratios only, so it is immune to supply drift.',
  'Each WS2812 contains three LEDs at roughly 20 mA per channel, so a fully lit white pixel draws about 60 mA. The controller inside also draws about 1 mA even when the LED is dark, which is easy to forget on a long strip: 300 pixels idle still costs around 300 mA.': 'Each WS2812 contains three LEDs at roughly 20 mA per channel, so a fully lit white pixel draws about 60 mA. The controller inside also draws about 1 mA even when the LED is dark, which is easy to forget on a long strip: 300 pixels idle still costs around 300 mA.',
  'each, 4 or more': 'each, 4 or more',
  'edge': 'edge',
  'Edges in window': 'Edges in window',
  'Efficiency': 'Efficiency',
  'Efficiency at full load': 'Efficiency at full load',
  'Efficiency is a first-order budget, not a simulation: `Irms²·DCR` in the winding, `Irms²·Rds(on)` in each FET weighted by its conduction time or `Vf·Iout·(1-D)` for a catch diode, plus hard switching loss `0.5·Vin·I·(tr+tf)·fsw` and the controller quiescent draw. Gate charge, core loss, dead time and layout parasitics are not modelled, so expect the real board to land a couple of points lower.': 'Efficiency is a first-order budget, not a simulation: `Irms²·DCR` in the winding, `Irms²·Rds(on)` in each FET weighted by its conduction time or `Vf·Iout·(1-D)` for a catch diode, plus hard switching loss `0.5·Vin·I·(tr+tf)·fsw` and the controller quiescent draw. Gate charge, core loss, dead time and layout parasitics are not modelled, so expect the real board to land a couple of points lower.',
  'Efficiency is dominated by two terms: the internal switch dropping about 1.16 V at 3 A, and the catch diode burning `Vf·Idiode` for the whole off-time. At low output voltages the diode conducts most of the period, which is why a 12 V to 3.3 V conversion is markedly less efficient than 12 V to 5 V.': 'Efficiency is dominated by two terms: the internal switch dropping about 1.16 V at 3 A, and the catch diode burning `Vf·Idiode` for the whole off-time. At low output voltages the diode conducts most of the period, which is why a 12 V to 3.3 V conversion is markedly less efficient than 12 V to 5 V.',
  'EIA-96 is the compact scheme for tiny 1% parts: two digits index into the 96 values of the E96 series, and a letter gives the multiplier. So 68C is the 68th E96 value, 499, times 100, i.e. 49.9 kΩ. It is dense but requires the table.': 'EIA-96 is the compact scheme for tiny 1% parts: two digits index into the 96 values of the E96 series, and a letter gives the multiplier. So 68C is the 68th E96 value, 499, times 100, i.e. 49.9 kΩ. It is dense but requires the table.',
