  'For a 32.768 kHz timekeeping crystal, 20 ppm is about 1.7 seconds a day, or ten minutes a year. If that matters, either trim the capacitors or use a temperature compensated module: temperature drift will typically dwarf the load error anyway, since a watch crystal has a parabolic tempco of about -0.035 ppm per °C squared.': 'For a 32.768 kHz timekeeping crystal, 20 ppm is about 1.7 seconds a day, or ten minutes a year. If that matters, either trim the capacitors or use a temperature compensated module: temperature drift will typically dwarf the load error anyway, since a watch crystal has a parabolic tempco of about -0.035 ppm per °C squared.',
