  'Headroom is the whole design question. Differentiating the loop equation gives `dI/dVf = -1/R`, so a {spread} bin-to-bin Vf shift changes the current by `0.1 / R` amps, i.e. by `0.1 / (Vs - Vf)` as a fraction. With a 3.2 V white LED on 3.3 V that is 100% of the current, which is exactly why white and blue parts want a driver rather than a resistor from 3.3 V.': 'Headroom is the whole design question. Differentiating the loop equation gives `dI/dVf = -1/R`, so a {spread} bin-to-bin Vf shift changes the current by `0.1 / R` amps, i.e. by `0.1 / (Vs - Vf)` as a fraction. With a 3.2 V white LED on 3.3 V that is 100% of the current, which is exactly why white and blue parts want a driver rather than a resistor from 3.3 V.',
