  'Simple adjustable supplies, current sources for LEDs and battery charging, and cases where switching noise is unacceptable. The critical output is dissipation: a linear regulator burns the voltage difference as heat, so 12 V to 3.3 V at 1 A means nearly 9 W and a heatsink, which is why a buck converter usually wins.': 'Simple adjustable supplies, current sources for LEDs and battery charging, and cases where switching noise is unacceptable. The critical output is dissipation: a linear regulator burns the voltage difference as heat, so 12 V to 3.3 V at 1 A means nearly 9 W and a heatsink, which is why a buck converter usually wins.',
