  'Wiring anything beyond a breadboard: battery leads, motor supplies, LED strip feeds, car and solar installations. The voltage drop figure is the useful one, since a supply that measures correctly at the source can arrive well below spec at the load, and the return conductor doubles the drop people usually calculate.': 'Wiring anything beyond a breadboard: battery leads, motor supplies, LED strip feeds, car and solar installations. The voltage drop figure is the useful one, since a supply that measures correctly at the source can arrive well below spec at the load, and the return conductor doubles the drop people usually calculate.',
  "With a sinusoidal supply, `S = Vrms·Irms`, `P = S·cos(phi)` and `Q = S·sin(phi)`. Only P does work. Q is energy shuttled into the load's magnetic field and back out every half cycle, and the cable carries it both ways.": "With a sinusoidal supply, `S = Vrms·Irms`, `P = S·cos(phi)` and `Q = S·sin(phi)`. Only P does work. Q is energy shuttled into the load's magnetic field and back out every half cycle, and the cable carries it both ways.",
  'With enough open-loop gain the inverting pin tracks the non-inverting pin, so the resistor network alone sets the gain: `Av = -Rf/Rin` inverting, `Av = 1 + Rf/Rg` non-inverting, `Av = 1` for a buffer. The summing amp is superposition on one virtual earth, `Vout = -Rf·(V1/R1 + V2/R2)`, and the difference amp is `Vout = Vref + (Rf/Rin)·(V+ - V-)` with matched ratios on both branches.': 'With enough open-loop gain the inverting pin tracks the non-inverting pin, so the resistor network alone sets the gain: `Av = -Rf/Rin` inverting, `Av = 1 + Rf/Rg` non-inverting, `Av = 1` for a buffer. The summing amp is superposition on one virtual earth, `Vout = -Rf·(V1/R1 + V2/R2)`, and the difference amp is `Vout = Vref + (Rf/Rin)·(V+ - V-)` with matched ratios on both branches.',
  'With nothing on the tap the current is the same in both legs, so `Vout = Vin·R2/(R1+R2)`. Only the ratio sets the voltage: 1k/1k and 1M/1M both give half the rail, but one wastes 1000x the current.': 'With nothing on the tap the current is the same in both legs, so `Vout = Vin·R2/(R1+R2)`. Only the ratio sets the voltage: 1k/1k and 1M/1M both give half the rail, but one wastes 1000x the current.',
  'Working out how current splits between parallel paths: paralleled resistors sharing power, LEDs unwisely paralleled on one resistor, and multiple return paths in a ground plane. It is also the model for why paralleling batteries or regulators without ballast leads to one of them doing all the work.': 'Working out how current splits between parallel paths: paralleled resistors sharing power, LEDs unwisely paralleled on one resistor, and multiple return paths in a ground plane. It is also the model for why paralleling batteries or regulators without ballast leads to one of them doing all the work.',
